 to add for you guys  what was it like reading the script for  the first time and because this is not  your typical narrative structure and  story yeah for me personally I think  that's the first thing that stood out  because  for young black actors we don't  typically get to do projects that have a  time-loop structure so when I read that  I was already blown away and then with  the content of it as well I was like oh  yeah this is this is something that  needs to be seen  thanks man I always say this it's like I  read it too fast because I mean Stan Lee  wrote such a beautiful story and I just  wanted to know what happened next I  never expected anything to happen I was  in the moment when I was reading it the  whole time and he wrote such beautiful  full characters so he made it like easy  for us because there wasn't much  guessing it was all it was mostly on the  page and and I mean he just wrote such a  beautiful script Spencer you have to  talk yeah I mean I think we all read  quite a few scripts and they don't  usually come along and have these kind  of well ideas and characters and well  let's be honest a lot of them are [ __ ]  [Laughter]  maybe we should not let Spencer you know  by the way you're not the only actor  I've heard say that they don't read like  the great scripts are hard to come by  yeah I hear that for truth you just  don't get that and especially from a 19  year old kid at the time you know it was  it was incredible and yeah I was just  happy to be able to get to go in and  read for it you know sure for for all of  you guys no matter what no matter what  movie you're making you never have  enough time and you never have enough  money so when you started to make this  thing for each of you what were you sort  of nervous to be able to pull off with  the time and money that you had I knew  that we had exactly enough time and  precisely enough money to do everything  we needed to do to land right here it  all worked out exactly as we planned  you're wrong okay you know the real  challenge of of the time and all you  know all the things that come with film  was just making sure that we had synergy  and that we were a family before we got  to set that was the real difficulty and  it worked out well you know I mean for  me just because the sort of work that  I've done in in television you know  you're always dealing with the schedule  you're always dealing with the tight  budget so it's kind of a training camp  you know for a production where you  don't have all the resources that you'd  like or need and there's a ticking clock  what helped us out in terms of  production is because it was a loop  structure that removed some of the work  of having to you know go through scene  by scene by scene and create new things  for every cycle although as we move  through the structure of the film we  advance the story by certain degrees but  you know everybody's still wearing the  same clothes so I mean most things make  things a little bit easy it's a  challenge but if you put your brains  together and really map out a plan as to  how you're gonna do it it doesn't work  out exactly but you can get pretty close  and I think we did actually gonna flip  the question for you guys because I just  realized you're probably filming  different structures of the time loop  maybe on the same day so what is it like  as actors trying to get your head around  the multiple versions of what you're  doing because you're filming on those  certain sets painful painful um it was  pretty tough keeping up with the time  loops because not only do we have to try  to navigate it in our own brains but we  have to make sure that we are painting  the picture for the audience so at any  given time you know we weren't shooting  in sequence so at one point we could be  at the climax of the film and then the  very next scene the same day it could be  the loop one where you know you're in a  completely different mindset we have a  completely different relationship we  have a completely different  relationships and so those things were  over overlapping every single day so  thank God for script supervisors  shout-out to them we need you  Holle shoutout and and Olli kept it  straight to I mean well I mean like the  little details for these guys it's like  when you're trying to build a  performance you know it's trying to make  sure that you start out at a place that  you can know alright here's my ground  zero right and and when you're at  something that's climactic or a big  moment you know you try to make it big  enough so it has enough distance  emotionally you know and performance  wise from where you started but you also  don't want it so far that it can't be  gotten to in the course of you know how  the story is structured how the story  flows Steven did an amazing job with  that he is I'm incredibly impressed by  sort of like his micro emotions like  he's able to convey a lot with a little  it's a film you have to pay attention to  no we don't spoon feed you anything you  know we're not we honestly don't help  and that also helped in terms of the way  it plays out because even the big  moments in a manner of speaking are kind  of small and that I think allowed him to  build a performance there was nuanced  but consistent and I think it kind of  gave a gauge to his other actors as well  yeah because a lot of my performance was  based on how because I didn't know how  he was gonna be within each loop you  know I can read it and anticipate all I  want but there was RIT was really like  almost an improv and improvisational  feel to a lot of the scenes because  we're shooting the same scene six  different ways kind of thing and little  things are changing in each one little  details again like an acting exercise  you know have you ever been to a camp  and they give you the same bones and  then you kind of have to play with it  differently so I feel also I mean for me  I learned so much by doing that and it  as 